1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * From the [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Altun_Jan_Khatun '''Wikipedia page'''] of his mother, Altun Jan Khatun:
2 CONT
2 CONT Before becoming Tughril Beg's wife she had been married to the Khwarezm Shah. Her son by that marriage, Anoushiravan, apparently remained with her after she married Tughril Beg.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT =References=
2 CONT
2 CONT * [1] Lambton 1988, p. 262.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT * From pages 262-263 of [https://books.google.com/books?id=lQjkJOUcVIwC&printsec=frontcover&source=gbs_ge_summary_r&cad=0#v=onepage&q=altun%20jan&f=false '''Continuity and Change in Medieval Persia'''] by Ann K.S. Lambton:
2 CONT
2 CONT When Toghril Beg went to Hamadan in 450/1058 to deal with the revolt of Ibrahim Inal, he sent his wife, Anushiravan and Kunduri to Baghdad (ibid., 31). Kunduri appears on this occasion to have considered replacing Toghril Beg by A
2 CONC nushiravan (see Makdisi, ''Ibn 'Aqil,'' 106ff., Glassen 48). Some years later in 455/1063, Anushiravan rebelled but was captured. He was imprisoned in Ray and killed after Toghril's death (Sibt b. al-Jauzi, ed. Semi, 100, 109). 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT * Anoushiravan was mentioned in a note on pages 145-146 of [https://books.google.com/books?id=BAN6ONlDkgIC&printsec=frontcover&source=gbs_ge_summary_r&cad=0#v=onepage&q&f=false '''The Annals of the Saljuq Turks: Selections from Al
2 CONC -Kamil Fi'l-Ta'rikh of Ibn Al-Athir'''], translated by D.S. Richards:
2 CONT
2 CONT The Kamil overlooks the story of Anushirwan, the step-son of Tughril Beg. As the death of the sultan looked likely, Anushirwan, made his second ill-fated bid for power. He was arrested, taken to Rayy, and later moved to a fortress
2 CONC  nearby (Mir'at, 100, II. 1-5). He tried to get the governor to release him. Mir'at, 109-10, tells the story:
2 CONT
2 CONT >After the sultan's death, he asked the governor to do what he had promised, but he would not. He wrote about him to Amid al-Mulk, who feared him and would not allow his release. Anushirwan was not quite right in the head. He invi
2 CONC ted the governor to come and sit with him to play chess in the room where he was held prisoner. Then he attacked and killed him. The garrison was in uproar and blockaded the room. Anushirwan feared for the servant girl who was wit
2 CONC h him, whom he loved. He said, 'Look out of this window at the desert. Look below the castle.' As she did so, he pushed her and threw her out, so that she would die before him, but the wind took her clothes and carried her to the 
2 CONC hillside. She broke her arm but survived. He jumped after her and was dashed to pieces. They carried him in a coffin and buried him by his mother."
