1 NOTE {geni:about_me} from Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Malik-Shah I''' (Turkish: Melikşah; Persian: ملکشاه; Arabic: معز الدنيا والدين جلال الدولة حسن ملكشاه‎; full name: Jalāl al-Dawla Mu'izz al-Dunyā Wa'l-Din Abu'l-Fatḥ ibn Alp Arslān[1]) (1055 – 19 November 1092) succeeded his fa
2 CONC ther, Alp Arslan, as the Sultan of the Seljuk Empire in 1072, and reigned until his death in 1092.[2][3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Although Malikshah was the nominal head of the Seljuq state, his famous vizier Nizam al-Mulk held near absolute power during his reign.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT =Contents=
2 CONT
2 CONT * 1 Early life
2 CONT * 2 Reign
2 CONT * 3 Death and aftermath
2 CONT * 4 Legacy
2 CONT * 5 Issue
2 CONT * 6 References
2 CONT * 7 Sources
2 CONT
2 CONT =Early life=
2 CONT
2 CONT Malik-Shah was born on 16 August 1055 and spent his youth in Isfahan. According to the 12th-century Persian historian Muhammad bin Ali Rawandi, Malik-Shah had fair skin, was tall and somewhat bulky.[5] In 1064, Malik-Shah took par
2 CONC t Alp Arslan’s campaign in the Caucasus. The same year, Malik-Shah was married to Terken Khatun, the daughter of the Kara-Khanid khan,[6] Ibrahim Tamghach-Khan.[7] In 458/1066, Alp Arslan arranged a ceremony near Merv, where he ap
2 CONC pointed Malik-Shah as his heir and also granted him Isfahan as a fief.[8][5]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 463/1071, Malik-Shah took part in the Syrian campaign of his father, and stayed in Aleppo when his father fought the Byzantine emperor Romanos IV Diogenes at Manzikert.[5] In 465/1072, Malik-Shah and Nizam al-Mulk accompanied A
2 CONC lp-Arslan during his campaign in Transoxiana against the Karakhanids. However, Alp-Arslan was badly wounded during his expedition, and Malik-Shah shortly took over the army. Alp-Arslan died some days later, and Malik-Shah was decl
2 CONC ared as the new sultan of the empire.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Reign=
2 CONT
2 CONT However, right after Malik-Shah accession, his uncle Qavurt claimed the throne for himself and sent Malik-Shah a message which said: "I am the eldest brother, and you are a youthful son; I have the greater right to my brother Alp-
2 CONC Arslan's inheritance." Malik-Shah then replied by sending the following message: "A brother does not inherit when there is a son.".[9] This message enraged Qavurt, who thereafter occupied Isfahan. In 1073 a battle took place near 
2 CONC Hamadan, which lasted three days. Qavurt was accompanied by his seven sons, and his army consisted of Turkmens, while the army of Malik-Shah consisted of ghulams ("military slaves") and contingents of Kurdish and Arab troops.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the battle, the Turks of Malik-Shah's army mutinied against him, but he nevertheless managed to defeat and capture Qavurt.[5][10] Qavurt then begged for mercy and in return promised to retire to Oman. However, Nizam al-Mulk
2 CONC  declined the offer, claiming that sparing him was an indication of weakness. After some time, Qavurt was strangled to death with a bowstring, while two of his sons were blinded. After having dealt with that problem, Malik-Shah ap
2 CONC pointed Qutlugh-Tegin as the governor of Fars and Sav-Tegin as the governor of Kerman.[11] Malik-Shah then turned his attention towards the Karakhanids, who had after the death of Alp-Arslan invaded Tukharistan, which was ruled by
2 CONC  Malik-Shah's brother Ayaz, who was unable to repel the Karakhanids and was killed by them. Malik-Shah eventually managed to repel the Karakhanids and captured Tirmidh, giving Sav-Tegin the key of the city. Malik-Shah then appoint
2 CONC ed his other brother Shihab al-Din Tekish as the ruler of Tukharistan and Balkh.[12] During the same period, the Ghaznavid ruler Ibrahim was seizing Seljuq territory in northern Khorasan, but was defeated by Malik-Shah, who then m
2 CONC ade peace with the latter and gave his daughter Gawhar Khatun in marriage to Ibrahim's son Mas'ud III.[13][5][14]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1074, Malik-Shah ordered the Turkic warlord Arghar to restore what he had destroyed during his raids in the territory of the Shirvanshah Fariburz I.[15] During the same year, he appointed Qavurt's son Rukn al-Dawla Sultan-Shah 
2 CONC as the ruler of Kerman.[11] One year later, Malik-Shah sent an army under Sav-Tegin to Arran, which was ruled by the Shaddadid ruler Fadlun III. Sav-Tegin managed to easily conquer the region, thus ending Shaddadid rule. Malik-Sha
2 CONC h then gave Gorgan to Fadlun III as a fief.[16] During the same year, the Jalali calendar was reformed[17] and throughout Malik's reign new institutions of learning were established throughout the Seljuq lands.[18] In 1089, Malik-
2 CONC Shah captured Samarkand with the support of the local clergy, and imprisoned its Karakhanid ruler Ahmad Khan ibn Khizr, who was the nephew of Turkan Khatun. He then marched to Semirechye, and made the Karakhanid Harun Khan ibn Sul
2 CONC ayman, who was the ruler of Kashgar and Khotan, acknowledge him as his suzerain.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1092 Nizam al-Mulk was assassinated near Sihna, on the road to Baghdad, by a man disguised as a Sufi.[19] As the assassin was immediately cut down by Nizam's bodyguard, it became impossible to establish with certainty who had s
2 CONC ent him. One theory had it that he was an Is'maili fanatic, since these regularly made attempts on the lives of Seljuq officials and rulers during the 11th century. Another theory had it that the attack had been instigated by Mali
2 CONC k-Shah, who may have grown tired of his overmighty vizier.[20] After Nizam al-Mulk's death, Malik-Shah appointed another Persian named Taj al-Mulk Abu'l Ghana'im as his vizier.[5] Malik-Shah then went to Baghdad and decided to dep
2 CONC ose al-Muqtadir and sent him the following message: "You must relinquish Baghdad to me, and depart to any land you choose." This was because Malik-Shah wanted to appoint his grandson (or nephew) Ja'far as the new Caliph.[5][21]
2 CONT
2 CONT =Death and aftermath=
2 CONT
2 CONT Malik-Shah died on 19 November 1092 while he was hunting. He was most likely poisoned by the Caliph or the supporters of Nizam al-Mulk. Under the orders of Turkan Khatun, Malik-Shah's body was taken back to Isfahan, where it was b
2 CONC uried in a madrasa.[5][22]
2 CONT
2 CONT Upon his death, the Seljuq Empire fell into chaos, as rival successors and regional governors carved up their empire and waged war against each other. The situation within the Seljuq lands was further complicated by the arrival of
2 CONC  the First Crusade, which detached large portions of Syria and Palestine from Muslim control in 1098 and 1099. The success of the First Crusade is at least in part attributable to the political confusion which resulted from Malik-
2 CONC Shah's death.[23]
2 CONT
2 CONT =Legacy=
2 CONT
2 CONT The 18th century English historian Edward Gibbon wrote of him:
2 CONT
2 CONT >On his father's death the inheritance was disputed by an uncle, a cousin, and a brother: they drew their cimeters, and assembled their followers; and the triple victory of Malek Shah established his own reputation and the right o
2 CONC f primogeniture. In every age, and more especially in Asia, the thirst of power has inspired the same passions, and occasioned the same disorders; but, from the long series of civil war, it would not be easy to extract a sentiment
2 CONC  more pure and magnanimous than is contained in the saying of the Turkish prince. On the eve of the battle, he performed his devotions at Thous, before the tomb of the Imam Riza. As the sultan rose from the ground, he asked his vi
2 CONC zier Nizam, who had knelt beside him, what had been the object of his secret petition: "That your arms may be crowned with victory," was the prudent, and most probably the sincere, answer of the minister. "For my part," replied th
2 CONC e generous Malek, "I implored the Lord of Hosts that he would take from me my life and crown, if my brother be more worthy than myself to reign over the Moslems." The favourable judgment of heaven was ratified by the caliph; and f
2 CONC or the first time, the sacred title of Commander of the Faithful was communicated to a Barbarian. But this Barbarian, by his personal merit, and the extent of his empire, was the greatest prince of his age.[24]
2 CONT
2 CONT =Issue=
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Zubeida Khatun and later, Terken Khatun. The known sons of Terken Khatun were Dawud, Abu Shuja Ahmad, and Mahmud I.
2 CONT
2 CONT * Rukn ad-Din Barkiyaruq
2 CONT * Ghiyath ad-Din Tapar
2 CONT * Muizz ad-Din Sanjar
2 CONT * Nasir ad-Din Mahmud
2 CONT * Adud ad-Dawlah Ahmad
2 CONT * Dawud
2 CONT * Princess Mah-i Mulk
2 CONT * Princess Sitara
2 CONT * Princess Gawhar Khatun
2 CONT
2 CONT =References=
2 CONT
2 CONT * [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Malik-Shah_I#References '''1-24''']
2 CONT
2 CONT =Sources=
2 CONT
2 CONT * [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Malik-Shah_I#Sources '''1-5''']
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Malik-Shah_I
2 CONT
2 CONT Jalāl al-Dawla Mu'izz al-Dunyā Wa'l-Din Abu'l-Fatḥ ibn Alp Arslān (8 August 1055 – 19 November 1092, full name: Persian: معزالدنیا و الدین ملکشاه بن محمد الب ارسلان قسیم امیرالمومنین‎[1]), better known by his regnal name of Malik-
2 CONC Shah I (Persian: ملکشاه‎, Turkish: Melikşah), was sultan of the Seljuk Empire from 1072 to 1092.
2 CONT
2 CONT During his youth, he spent his time participating in the campaigns of his father Alp Arslan, along the latter's vizier Nizam-al-Mulk https://www.geni.com/people/Nizam-al-Mulk/6000000032959387338 . During one of such campaigns in 1
2 CONC 072, Alp Arslan was fatally wounded and died only a few days later. After that, Malik-Shah was crowned as the new sultan of the empire, however, Malik-Shah did not access the throne peacefully, and had to fight his uncle Qavurt, w
2 CONC ho claimed the throne. Although Malik-Shah was the nominal head of the Seljuk state, Nizam al-Mulk held near absolute power during his reign.[2] Malik-Shah spent the rest of his reign waging war against the Karakhanids on the east
2 CONC ern side, and establishing order in the Caucasus.
2 CONT
2 CONT Malik-Shah's death to this day remains under dispute; according to some scholars, he was poisoned by Abbasid caliph al-Muqtadi, while others say that he was poisoned by the supporters of Nizam al-Mulk.  lthough he was known by sev
2 CONC eral names, he was mostly known as "Malik-Shah", a combination of the Arabic word malik (king) and the Persian word shah (which also means king). Early life
2 CONT
2 CONT Malik-Shah was born on 16 August 1055 and spent his youth in Isfahan. According to the 12th-century Persian historian Muhammad bin Ali Rawandi, Malik-Shah had fair skin, was tall and somewhat bulky.[3] In 1064, Malik-Shah, only 9 
2 CONC years old by then, along with Nizam al-Mulk, the Persian vizier of the Empire,[4] took part in Alp Arslan’s campaign in the Caucasus. The same year, Malik-Shah was married to Terken Khatun, the daughter of the Karakhanid khan Ibra
2 CONC him Tamghach-Khan.[3] 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1066, Alp Arslan arranged a ceremony near Merv, where he appointed Malik-Shah as his heir and also granted him Isfahan as a fief.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1071, Malik-Shah took part in the Syrian campaign of his father, and stayed in Aleppo when his father fought the Byzantine emperor Romanos IV Diogenes at Manzikert.[3] In 1072, Malik-Shah and Nizam al-Mulk accompanied Alp-Arsla
2 CONC n during his campaign in Transoxiana against the Karakhanids. However, Alp-Arslan was badly wounded during his expedition, and Malik-Shah shortly took over the army. Alp-Arslan died some days later, and Malik-Shah was declared as 
2 CONC the new sultan of the empire.  ...
