2 CONC Chaghri-Beg, who was the wife of Abbasid caliph Al-Qa'im. Perhaps Mahmud II was married to two women named Ata Khatun?
2 CONT
2 CONT * from [https://books.google.com/books?id=lQjkJOUcVIwC&pg=PA35&lpg=PA35&dq=Zubeida+Khatun,+wife+of+Malik-Shah&source=bl&ots=5h6pZ2rimG&sig=reGhqSBaBsqKwuYCVdSrdzoi_6I&hl=en&sa=X&ei=vWwyVdW8EMnksAT_mIDwBQ&ved=0CDEQ6AEwAw#v=onepage&
2 CONC q=Aka&f=false '''Continuity and Change in Medieval Persia'''] by Ann K.S. Lambton:
2 CONT
2 CONT Garchasp apparently had another daughter Ata Khatun, who was married to Mahmud b. Muhammad. They had a son Ala al-Daula Ata Khan, who came back to Yazd with his mother Ata Khatun after the death of Mahmud...
2 CONT
2 CONT When Ata Khan and Ata Khatun came to Yazd, they brought with them Terken Khatun, the daughter of Mahmud b. Muhammad, whose mother was a concubine. After the death of Ata Khatun, one of Qavurt’s grandsons asked the hand of Terken K
2 CONC hatun for his son Sulaiman-Shah.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''NOTE''': In the same source, Ata Khatun is listed as the daughter of Garchasp's father, thus making her his sister and not his own daughter. This comes from the family tree on [https://books.google.com/books?id=lQjkJOUcVIwC&pg=
2 CONC PA35&lpg=PA35&dq=Zubeida+Khatun,+wife+of+Malik-Shah&source=bl&ots=5h6pZ2rimG&sig=reGhqSBaBsqKwuYCVdSrdzoi_6I&hl=en&sa=X&ei=vWwyVdW8EMnksAT_mIDwBQ&ved=0CDEQ6AEwAw#v=onepage&q=Aka&f=false '''page 390'''].
2 CONT
2 CONT Ata Khatun is also named the daughter of Ali ibn Faramarz on the [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seljuk_Empire#List_of_sultans_of_the_Great_Seljuq_Empire '''List of Seljuq Sultans and Sultanas'''] on Wikipedia.
