1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/B%C3%B6ritigin
2 CONT
2 CONT Böritigin, also known as Ibrahim ibn Nasr[1] or Tamghach Khan Ibrahim,[2] was a Karakhanid ruler in Transoxiana from 1038 to 1068. He was one of the greatest rulers of the dynasty.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the son of Nasr Khan, a Karakhanid ruler from the western branch of the family, known as the "Alids", which was named after their ancestor Ali ibn Musa Qara Khan.
2 CONT
2 CONT Böritigin is first mentioned during some time after 1034, when was imprisoned by the sons of the Karakhanid deceased Ali-Tegin, who was from the eastern branch of the family, known as the "Hasanids". However, Böritigin eventually 
2 CONC managed in 1038 to escape to his brother who was at Uzgend,[3] and then raised an army consisting of Kumiji tribes from the upper Oxus river.[4] He then invaded the territories of the Ghaznavid Sultan Mas'ud I, and plundered Khutt
2 CONC al and Vakhsh.[3] He then conquered Chaghaniyan and defeated a Ghaznavid counter-attack.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT The following year, Böritgin began fighting the sons of Ali-Tegin and by 1040 had annexed much of Transoxiana. He then made Samarkand his capital and took the title of "Tamghach Khan Ibrahim".[3] In 1042, the newly crowned Ghaznav
2 CONC id ruler Maw'dud invaded the territories of the Seljuqs and conquered much of Khorasan. This greatly increased the fame of Maw'dud and made Böritigin acknowledge him as his suzerain.[6] In ca. 1050, Maw'dud, with the aid Böritigin
2 CONC  and an army sent by the former Kakuyid ruler Garshasp I re-invaded Khorasan; Böritigin and his commander Qashgha invaded Khwarazm and Tirmidh, however, during the invasion, Maw'dud died and thus the invasion failed. The Seljuqs t
2 CONC hen extended their rule as far as Vakhsh and appointed a certain Abu 'Ali ibn Shadhan as the governor of their new conquests.[7] After this, Böritigin seems to have stopped recognizing the Ghaznavids as his suzerain.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1059/60 Böritigin forced the Karakhanid rulers of Farghana to acknowledge him as their suzerain.[3] In the early 1060s, the newly crowned Seljuq ruler Alp Arslan invaded Transoxiana, which made Böritigin complain to the Abbasid
2 CONC  caliph of needless aggression from the Seljuqs.[8]
2 CONT
2 CONT Böritigin later died in 1068 and was succeeded by his son Shams al-Mulk Nasr.[9] He also had a daughter named Terken Khatun who later married the son of Alp Arslan, Malik-Shah I. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT * from [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/B%C3%B6ritigin '''Wikipedia'''], the free encyclopedia.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Böritigin''', also known as '''Tamghach Khan Ibrahim''', was a Karakhanid ruler in Transoxiana from 1038 to 1068. He was one of the greatest ruler of the dynasty.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Contents=
2 CONT
2 CONT * 1 Biography
2 CONT * 2 References
2 CONT * 3 Sources
2 CONT * 4 Further reading
2 CONT
2 CONT =Biography=
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the son of Nasr Khan, a Karakhanid ruler from the western branch of the family, known as the "Alids", which was named after their ancestor Ali ibn Musa Qara Khan.
2 CONT
2 CONT Böritigin is first mentioned during some time after 1034, when was imprisoned by the sons of the Karakhanid deceased Ali-Tegin, who was from the eastern branch of the family, known as the "Hasanids". However, Böritigin eventually 
2 CONC managed in 1038 to escape to his brother who was at Uzgend,[1] and then raised an army consisting of Kumiji tribes from the upper Oxus river.[2] He then invaded the territories of the Ghaznavid Sultan Mas'ud I, and plundered Khutt
2 CONC al and Vakhsh.[1] He then conquered Chaghaniyan and defeated a Ghaznavid counter-attack.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT The following year, Böritgin began fighting the sons of Ali-Tegin and by 1040 had annexed much of Transoxiana. He then made Samarkand his capital and took the title of "Tamghach Khan Ibrahim".[1] In 1042, the newly crowned Ghaznav
2 CONC id ruler Maw'dud invaded the territories of the Seljuqs and conquered much of Khorasan. This greatly increased the fame of Maw'dud and made Böritigin acknowledge him as his suzerain.[4] In ca. 1050, Maw'dud, with the aid Böritigin
2 CONC  and an army sent by the former Kakuyid ruler Garshasp I re-invaded Khorasan; Böritigin and his commander Qashgha invaded Khwarazm and Tirmidh, however, during the invasion, Maw'dud died and thus the invasion failed. The Seljuqs t
2 CONC hen extended their rule as far as Vakhsh and appointed a certain Abu 'Ali ibn Shadhan as the governor of their new conquests.[5] After this, Böritigin seems to have stopped recognizing the Ghaznavids as his suzerain.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1059/60 Böritigin forced the Karakhanid rulers of Farghana to acknowledge him as their suzerain.[1] In the early 1060s, the newly crowned Seljuq ruler Alp Arslan invaded Transoxiana, which made Böritigin complain to the Abbasid
2 CONC  caliph of needless aggression from the Seljuqs.[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT Böritigin later died in 1068 and was succeeded by his son Shams al-Mulk Nasr.[7] He also had a daughter named Terken Khatun who later married the son of Alp Arslan, Malik-Shah I.
2 CONT
