1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==[https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kata_of_Georgia Kata of Georgia, Wikipedia]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Kata''' or '''Katai''' (Georgian: კატა, კატაჲ) was a daughter of David IV, King of Georgia. She was married off by his father into the Byzantine imperial family c. 1116, but the identity of her husband is not revealed in the me
2 CONC dieval sources. There are three modern hypotheses regarding her marriage.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT =Contents=
2 CONT
2 CONT * 1 Marriage
2 CONT * 1.1 Medieval chronicle
2 CONT * 1.2 Modern theories
2 CONT * 2 See also
2 CONT * 3 References
2 CONT
2 CONT =Marriage=
2 CONT
2 CONT =Medieval Chronicle=
2 CONT
2 CONT Kata's marriage is mentioned by the 12th-century History of the King of Kings David, part of the compiled Georgian Chronicles, which does not specify the name of his husband.[2] The chronicle extols Kata and her sister, Tamar, a w
2 CONC ife of the shah of Shirvan, as luminaries of the West and the East, respectively, reflecting the splendor of their father.[3][4]
2 CONT
2 CONT =Modern Theories=
2 CONT
2 CONT There are different scholarly opinions as to who exactly was Kata’s spouse. According to a hypothesis commonly accepted by historians in Georgia,[5] she married Isaac Komnenos, the third son of the emperor Alexios I Komnenos,[6] a
2 CONC nd became Irene, a name recorded by the Byzantine sources as that of Isaac's wife. If the hypothesis is true and Helene, a daughter of Isaac and Kata, was indeed the wife of the Rurikid Rus' prince Yuri Dolgorukiy, then it may pro
2 CONC vide, through descent from antiquity, a Bagratid ancestry to numerous Russian and Polish descendants.[5] Alternatively, Isaac's wife Irene may have been the same person as an anonymous daughter of Volodar of Peremyshl known from t
2 CONC he Slavonic Primary Chronicle to have married the likewise unnamed son of the emperor Alexios.[4][5][7]
2 CONT
2 CONT Another version given currency, among other scholars, by Cyril Toumanoff, holds that Kata was married to the megas doux Alexios Bryennios, a son of Nikephoros Bryennios the Younger and Anna Komnena. The third hypothesis, supported
2 CONC  by Paul Gautier and Mihail-Dimitri Sturdza, states that Kata's husband was Alexios Komnenos, the eldest son of the Byzantine emperor John II Komnenos.[1] The Byzantine chronicle of John Zonaras mentions the arrival of the Georgia
2 CONC n (Abasgian) bride of the elder son of John II at Constantinople immediately after his accession to the throne, not long after 1118.[4][5][8]
2 CONT
2 CONT =See also=
2 CONT
2 CONT * [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Family_of_David_IV_of_Georgia '''Family of David IV of Georgia''']
2 CONT
2 CONT =References=
2 CONT
2 CONT # Lynda Garland & Stephen Rapp. Mary 'of Alania': Woman and Empress Between Two Worlds, p. 121. In: Lynda Garland (ed., 2006), Byzantine Women: Varieties of Experience, 800-1200. Ashgate Publishing, Ltd., ISBN 0-7546-5737-X.
2 CONT # Toumanoff, Cyril (July 1940), "On the Relationship between the Founder of the Empire of Trebizond and the Georgian Queen Thamar", Speculum, Vol. 15, No. 3: p. 307, fn. 2.
2 CONT # (French) Brosset, Marie-Félicité, (1849) Histoire de la Géorgie de l'Antiquité au XIXe siècle, p. 360. St. Petersbourg: A la typographie de l'Academie Impériale des Sciences.
2 CONT # Prinke, Rafał T. (2011), "Kata of Georgia", Foundations 3 (6): 489-502.
2 CONT # (Polish) Prinke, Rafał T. (1998), Krew Bagratydów. Genealogiczne związki Rurykowiczów i Komnenów z Bagratydami w XII wieku. in: Nuntius Vetustatis, sive Opuscula diversa Professori Georgio Wisłocki septuagenario dedicata, Posnan
2 CONC iae, Anno Domini MCMXCVIII.
2 CONT # Suny, Ronald Grigor (1994), The Making of the Georgian Nation, p. 36. Indiana University Press, ISBN 0253209153.
2 CONT #  Magdalino, Paul (2002), The Empire of Manuel I Komnenos, 1143–1180, p. 205. Cambridge University Press. ISBN 0-521-52653-1.
2 CONT # Kazhdan, Alexander & Franklin, Simon (1984), Studies on Byzantine literature of the eleventh and twelfth centuries, p. 94. Cambridge University Press, ISBN 273510043X.
2 CONT
2 CONT
