1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Sources needed to support as daughter of https://www.geni.com/people/Shahriyar-II-of-the-Bavandids/6000000032998172042?through=6000000002188294210
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT * from the [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Khosrovanuysh '''Wikipedia'''] page for Khosrovanuysh
2 CONT
2 CONT Khosrovanuysh (Armenian: Խոսրովանույշ) was a fifth queen of [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bagratid_Armenia Bagratid Armenia], and wife of Armenian king [https://www.geni.com/people/Ashot-III-the-Peaceful-king-of-Armenia/600000000
2 CONC 2188294202?through=6000000002188294210 Ashot III].
2 CONT
2 CONT Queen Khosrovanuysh was a great philanthropist in medieval Armenia. 
2 CONT
2 CONT *It is known also that her daughter [https://www.geni.com/people/Hripsime-of-Armenia/6000000031830485829?through=6000000002188294210 Hripsime] became a nun.[1] 
2 CONT
2 CONT There is little information about the queen's ancestors and lineage, also unknown when she was born, but the names of her children and grandchildren have been preserved. 
2 CONT
2 CONT She had 3 sons: 
2 CONT *[https://www.geni.com/people/Smbat-II-of-Armenia/6000000031830234063?through=6000000002188294210 Smbat],
2 CONT *[https://www.geni.com/people/Gagik-I-the-Great-king-of-Armenia/6000000002188294179?through=6000000031830234063 Gagik] and 
2 CONT *[https://www.geni.com/people/Kiurike-I-of-Lori/6000000031830202218?through=6000000002188294179 Gurgen]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Smbat inherited his father's throne and became the Armenian king (977-990), who was succeeded by Gagik (990-1020). Gurgen founded a separate royal branch in Lori (Kiurikian) and became first king of Kingdom of Tashir-Dzoraget.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Khosrovanuysh with the help of her husband King Ashot III Bagratuni began in 966 the construction of the Church of the Savior which she dedicated to her sons Smbat and Gurgen who are shown on a bas relief of the tympanum.[3] In th
2 CONC e 14th year of Ashot III reign, in 966, Queen Khosrovanuysh built the Sanahin Monastery,[4][5]and then, in 976, the Haghpatavank.[6][7][8] They became scientific and educational centers in the Middle Ages, a university and a libra
2 CONC ry were opened in Sanahin. The Cathedral of Surb Nshan (Holy sign) also was bulit around 967-976 by queen.[9][10]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT * from the [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ashot_III_of_Armenia '''Wikipedia'''] page of her husband, Ashot III of Armenia:
2 CONT
2 CONT In 961 Ani was proclaimed the capital of the kingdom, and Ashot set himself to enriching and expanding the city. Ashot constructed a wall enclosing Ani and that would later take its name after him, and sponsored the building of mo
2 CONC nasteries, hospitals, schools, and almshouses. His consort, Queen Khosrovanuysh, meanwhile sponsored the construction of the churches in Sanahin and Haghpat.[4]
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT ===References 
2 CONT #https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Tabaristan-1
2 CONT # “Armenian Queens” (January 30, 2020) 
2 CONT # https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tabaristan was a mountainous region located on the Caspian coast of northern Iran. It corresponded to the present-day province of Mazandaran, which became the predominant name of the area from the 1
2 CONC 1th-century onwards.[1]
2 CONT # https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mazanderani_people Mazanderani people are also known as the Tabari people, and traditionally call the Mazanderani language as Tabari. Their region was called Tapuria or Tapurestan, Land of Tapuris.[
2 CONC 8] Most Mazanderanis live on the southeastern coast of Caspian Sea. Their traditional professions are farming and fishing.[1] Mazandaranis are closely related to the neighboring Gilaki people as well as South Caucasian peoples (e.
2 CONC g. Georgians, Armenians).[9][10]
2 CONT # https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bagratuni_dynasty The Bagratuni or Bagratid dynasty (Armenian: Բագրատունի, Armenian pronunciation: [bagɾatuni]) was an Armenian royal dynasty which ruled the medieval Kingdom of Armenia from c. 885 
2 CONC until 1045.
2 CONT # https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bagratuni_family_tree The Bagratuni family tree describes the heritage of the Bagratuni family in Armenia (Bagratuni dynasty). Bagratuni lineage also lays claim to the Georgian Bagrationi dynasty.
