1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * from [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mas%27ud_I_of_Ghazni '''Wikipedia'''], the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Mas'ud I of Ghazni''' (Persian: مسعود غزنوی‎), known as '''Amīr-i Shahīd''' (امیر شهید; "the martyr king"), was sultan of the Ghaznavid Empire from 1030 to 1040. He rose to power by seizing the Ghaznavid throne from his younger
2 CONC  twin Mohammad, who had been nominated as the heir upon the death of their father Mahmud of Ghazni. His twin was shortly blinded and imprisoned. However, when much of Mas'ud's western domains had been wrested from his control, his
2 CONC  troops rebelled against him and reinstated Mohammad to the throne.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Contents=
2 CONT
2 CONT * 1 Early life
2 CONT * 1.1 Campaigns
2 CONT * 1.2 Struggle for the throne
2 CONT * 2 Reign
2 CONT * 2.1 Consolation of the Empire and war with the Kara-Khanids
2 CONT * 2.2 War in western Iran and clash with Turkic nomads
2 CONT * 2.3 War with the Seljuqs and downfall
2 CONT * 2.4 Death and aftermath
2 CONT * 3 References
2 CONT * 4 Sources
2 CONT
2 CONT =Early life=
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Campaigns==
2 CONT
2 CONT Mas'ud was born along with his younger twin brother Mohammad in 998 at the Ghaznavid capital of Ghazni. In 1015, Mas'ud was appointed as heir of the Ghaznavid Empire by his father, and was also appointed as the governor of Herat. 
2 CONC Five years later, he led an expedition in Ghur, which was still a pagan enslave. Mas'ud later participated in the campaigns of his father in Jibal, where they managed to annex the Buyid amirate of Ray which was then under the rule
2 CONC  of Majd al-Dawla.
2 CONT
2 CONT After Mas'ud's father left the region, Mas'ud was in charge of the Ghaznavid operations in western Iran; he continued his campaigns further west, where he managed to defeat the Kakuyid ruler Muhammad ibn Rustam Dushmanziyar, who m
2 CONC ade a treaty where he agreed to recognize Ghaznavid authority.
2 CONT
2 CONT However, Muhammad kept violating the treaty, and in 1030 wrested Ray from the Ghaznavids. During the same period, Mahmud, because of his bad relations with Mas'ud, changed his opinion, and appointed Mohammad as his heir,[1] who wa
2 CONC s much more less experienced in government and military affairs than Mas'ud. Mahmud shortly died, and was succeeded by Mohammad.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Struggle for the throne==
2 CONT
2 CONT However, his uncle Yusuf ibn Sabuktigin, and the Ghaznavid army including prominent officers such as Ali Daya, were in favor of Mas'ud, whose military campaigns had earned him a great reputation.[1] Mas'ud was also joined by his f
2 CONC ormer assistant Abu Sahl Zawzani, who in the words of the historian Yusofi, "became a sort of vizier and rose in prestige and influence. He also became feared, since he exercised his bent toward vengefulness, spite, and intrigue".
2 CONC [2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Nevertheless, in order to further strengthen his army, Mas'ud recruited a group of Turkmens which were head by their chiefs Yaghmur, Qizil, Bogha and Goktash.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT =Reign=
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Consolation of the Empire and war with the Kara-Khanids==
2 CONT
2 CONT Mas'ud then marched towards Ghazni, where he defeated his brother and had him imprisoned, while crowning himself as the new Sultan of the Ghaznavid Empire. Mas'ud shortly released the disgraced statesman Ahmad Maymandi from prison
2 CONC , and appointed him as his vizier. He also appointed Ali Daya as the commander-in-chief of the army of Khorasan, while another general named Ahmad Inaltigin was appointed as the commander-in-chief of the army in India.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Although Mas'ud was a great military leader, he was heedless in advice from his officers, which would later result in disastrous events during his reign.[5] He also suspected the majority of his father's officers of treachery, and
2 CONC  even had his own uncle Yusuf imprisoned, and also had the powerful statesman Ali ibn Il-Arslan imprisoned. In 1032, Ahmad Maymandi died and was succeeded by Ahmad Shirazi as Mas'ud's vizier. Sometime later, Mas'ud's governor and 
2 CONC de facto ruler of Khwarazm, Altun Tash, was sent to invade the domains of the Kara-Khanid ruler Ali Tigin Bughra Khan, but was killed at Dabusiyya, a town near Samarkand. He was then succeeded by his son Harun.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==War in western Iran and clash with Turkic nomads==
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1033, Mas'ud captured the fortress of Sarsut, and shortly invaded Kerman, which was then under the rule of the Buyid ruler Abu Kalijar. Mas'ud shortly managed to conquer the region, however, the inhabitants of Kerman, who prefe
2 CONC rred Buyid rule, rallied to Abu Kalijar, and under his vizier Bahram ibn Mafinna re-conquered Kerman.[6] During the same period, Ahmad Inaltigin rebelled and defeated an army sent by Mas'ud, who shortly sent another army under an 
2 CONC Indian statesman named Tilak, who managed to rout Ahmad Inaltigin, who drowned while he was trying to escape from Tilak.[4][5] In 1033, Mas'ud married the daughter of the Ziyarid Anushirvan Sharaf al-Ma'ali's relative Abu Kalijar,
2 CONC  who was the real ruler of the Ziyarid state. During the same year, in order to keep control over his vassal unreliable the Kakuyid ruler Muhammad, Mas'ud I appointed Abu Sahl Hamduwi as the governor of Jibal.[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1034, Harun declared independence from the Ghaznavids, and allied himself with the Kara-Khanid ruler Ali Tigin.[8] Mas'ud, however, managed to have Harun assassinated, and Ali Tigin shortly himself died. Harun was succeeded by 
2 CONC his brother Ismail Khandan, who continued to ally with the Kara-Khanids. Meanwhile, the Seljuq Turks under the leadership of Tughril, asked Mas'ud for asylum. Mas'ud, however, considered the Turkic nomads a dangerous threat and se
2 CONC nt an army under Begtoghdi, who was the newly appointed commander-in-chief in Khorasan. The army was shortly defeated by the Seljuqs, who forced Mas'ud to cede Nasa, Farava and Dihistan in return for Seljuq recognition of Ghaznavi
2 CONC d authority. In 1034, Mas'ud marched an army to Amul to collect tribute, sacking Amul for four days and later burned it to the ground.[9]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1035, Mas'ud I made another invasion of western Iran, where he defeated Abu Kalijar who had rebelled against him. Mas'ud then marched towards Jibal, where he once again defeated the Kakuyid ruler Muhammad, who fled to the Buyid
2 CONC s of Ahvaz, and then to northwestern Iran, where he raised an army composing of Turkmens. In 1037/8, while Mas'ud was campaigning in India, Muhammad, at the head of an Turkmen army, once again occupied Ray from the Ghaznavids. Mea
2 CONC nwhile, another Kara-Khanid ruler named Böritigin, invaded the Ghaznavid territories and plundered Khuttal and Vakhsh.[10] He also managed to conquer Chaghaniyan and repel the local Muhtajid dynasty from the region. Furthermore, t
2 CONC he Seljuqs had begun to slowly subdue the cities of Khorasan, and when they captured Nishapur, Tughril proclaimed himself as the ruler of Khorasan. Mas'ud, after having returned to Khorasan, tried to re-conquer Chaghaniyan but was
2 CONC  defeated by Böritigin.[11]
2 CONT
2 CONT ==War with the Seljuqs and downfall==
2 CONT
2 CONT However, he expelled the Seljuqs from Herat and Nishapur. He soon marched towards Merv to completely remove the Seljuq threat from Khorasan. His army included 50,000 men and 60 or 12 war elephants. He was accompanied by his vizier
2 CONC  Ahmad Shirazi, his chief secretary Abu Sahl Zawzani, his generals Ali Daya, Begtoghdi and Subashi, and Abd al-Razzaq Maymandi, the son of Ahmad Maymandi.
2 CONT
2 CONT A battle shortly took place near Merv, known as the Battle of Dandanaqan, where the army of Mas'ud was defeated by a much smaller army under Tughril, his brother Chaghri Beg, and the Kakuyid prince Faramurz. Mas'ud thus permanentl
2 CONC y lost control of all of western Khorasan. Although Mas'ud managed to retain his capital Ghazni, he chose to leave the city, and set up a capital in India. Mas'ud, who blamed Ali Daya and other generals for the disastrous Ghaznavi
2 CONC d defeat near Merv, had them imprisoned in India. However, the army of Mas'ud, which used to greatly hold him in high esteem, revolted against him, and had his brother Mohammad reinstated to the throne.[12]
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Death and aftermath==
2 CONT
2 CONT Mohammad then had Mas'ud imprisoned at Giri, where he was killed either on the orders of Mohammad or Mohammad's son Ahmed.[13] Mas'ud had a son named Maw'dud Ghaznavi, who later avenged his father by killing Mohammad, and then cro
2 CONC wned himself as the new ruler of the Ghaznavid Empire. He also had other sons named Sa'id, Izad-yar, Mardan-shah, Majdud, Ibrahim, Ali, and Farrukh-Zad. The last three sons also managed to later become the ruler of the Ghaznavid E
2 CONC mpire in different periods.
2 CONT
2 CONT =References=
2 CONT
2 CONT * [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mas%27ud_I_of_Ghazni#References '''1-13''']
2 CONT
2 CONT =Sources=
2 CONT
2 CONT * [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mas%27ud_I_of_Ghazni#Sources '''1-8''']
