1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * The [https://books.google.com/books?id=16yHq5v3QZAC&pg=PA170&lpg=PA170&dq=Inanj+Sonqur&source=bl&ots=1KiidLPl__&sig=iwp6o97ZkT6PHnw4kRXTDaUE1BE&hl=en&sa=X&ved=0ahUKEwi4h4TvtufOAhUHXhoKHQgOBxEQ6AEINTAE#v=onepage&q
2 CONC =Inanj&f=false '''Cambridge History of Iran, Vol. 5'''], by J.A. Boyle records his parentage and close family:
2 CONT
2 CONT The north-west provinces of Iran remained quite outside the sultans' sphere of direct influence. Power here was divided between the Eldiguzids and the Ahmadilis. Shams al-Din Eldiguz (d. 570/1174-5 or possibly 571/1175-6) was orig
2 CONC inally a slave of Sultan Mahmud's vizier, al-Kamal al-Simirumi; then he passed into the possession of Sultan Mas'ud, who appointed him governor of Arran. For sometime he kept aloof from the quarrels over the sultanate, until the f
2 CONC ortunate marriage which he had made with Toghril's widow Mu'mine Khatun enabled him to champion the succession of Arslan b. Toghril; the latter he duly set up at Hamadan on the murder of Sulaiman-Shah in 556/1161. His son Nusrat a
2 CONC l-Din Pahlavan Muhammad was Sultan Arslans's half-brother, and Pahlavan succeeded not only to the paternal territories in Arran and much of Azerbaijan, but also to Jibal, Isfahan, and Ray, with his brother Qizil-Arslan 'Uthman rul
2 CONC ing in Tabriz as his subordinate. 
