1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Bar Hebraeus (b.1226- d.1286) assures us that she became a muslim. 
2 CONT -----
2 CONT
2 CONT * from [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rusudan_of_Georgia '''Wikipedia'''], the free encyclopedia.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Queen Rusudan''' (Georgian: რუსუდანი) (c. 1194–1245), from the Bagrationi dynasty, ruled Georgia in 1223–1245.
2 CONT
2 CONT Daughter of Queen Tamar of Georgia by David Soslan, she succeeded her brother George IV of Georgia on January 18, 1223. George’s untimely death marked the beginning of the end of the Georgian “golden age”. Rusudan was too weak to 
2 CONC preserve whatever was gained by her predecessors.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the autumn of 1225, Georgia was attacked by the Khwarezmian shah Jelal ad-Din Mingburnu, pursued by the Mongols. The Georgians suffered bitter defeat at the Battle of Garni, and the royal court with Queen Rusudan moved to Kutai
2 CONC si, when the Georgian capital Tbilisi was besieged by the Khwarezmians. A year later Jelal ad-Din took Tbilisi on 9 March 1226. The citizens fought courageously and over 100,000 lost their lives when the city fell to the Khwarezmi
2 CONC ans. The defeated Georgians were ordered to change religion and become Muslims, but refused and almost the whole population of Tbilisi was massacred. In February 1227, the Georgians took advantage of Jelal ad-Din’s failures in Arm
2 CONC enia, and retook Tbilisi, but soon were forced to abandon the city – which they themselves had set alight in their battle with the occupation forces. Rusudan made an alliance with the neighbouring Seljuk rulers of Rüm and Ahlat, b
2 CONC ut the Georgians were routed by the Khwarezmians at Bolnisi, before the allies could arrive (1228).
2 CONT
2 CONT The Khwarezmians were superseded by the Mongols. They advanced into Georgia in 1235. Devastated and plundered by Jelal ad-Din’s incursions, Georgia surrendered without any serious resistance. By 1240 all the country was under the 
2 CONC Mongol yoke. Forced to accept the sovereignty of the Mongol Khan in 1242, Rusudan had to pay an annual tribute of 50,000 gold pieces and support the Mongols with a Georgian army.
2 CONT
2 CONT Fearing that her nephew David VII of Georgia would aspire to the throne, Rusudan held him prisoner at the court of her son-in-law, the sultan Kaykhusraw II, and sent her son David VI of Georgia to the Mongol court to get his offic
2 CONC ial recognition as heir apparent. She died in 1245, still waiting for her son to return.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Marriage and children=
2 CONT
2 CONT She married in 1224 to the Seljuk prince Ghias ad-din, a grandson of Kilij Arslan II who converted to Christianity on his marriage. They were the parents of David VI of Georgia and a daughter named Tamar, who married her cousin, t
2 CONC he sultan Kaykhusraw II, and following his death in 1246, the Pervane Mu'in al-Din Suleyman as one of the preconditions of the peace settlement.
2 CONT
2 CONT =See also=
2 CONT
2 CONT * [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_Georgian_monarchs '''List of Georgian monarchs''']
2 CONT * [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bagrationi_dynasty '''Bagrationi dynasty''']
2 CONT
2 CONT =References=
2 CONT
2 CONT * Wilhelm Baum (2007). "Rusudani (Russutuna)". In Bautz, Traugott. Biographisch-Bibliographisches Kirchenlexikon (BBKL) (in German) 27. Nordhausen: Bautz. cols. 1179–1184. ISBN 978-3-88309-393-2.
2 CONT --------------------
