1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==نور الدين محمود زنگي (Nūr al-Dīn Maḥmūd Zengī) (Feb 1118 – 15 May 1174, bur: Nur al-Din Madrasa, Damascus, Syria)
2 CONT s/o Imad al-Din Zeng
2 CONT x Ismat ad-Din Khatun
2 CONT *As-Salih Ismail al-Malik
2 CONT
2 CONT Regnal name: al-Malik al-Adil Abul-Qasim Nur ad-Din Mahmud Ibn 'Imad ad-Din Zengi
2 CONT Dynasty	Zengid dynasty
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nur_al-Din_Zengi
2 CONT
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000216938713826&size=large}} 
2 CONT ----
2 CONT Nur ad-Din was the second son of Imad ad-Din Zengi, the Turkic atabeg of Aleppo and Mosul, who was a devoted enemy of the crusader presence in Syria. After the assassination of his father in 1146, Nur ad-Din and his older brother 
2 CONC Saif ad-Din Ghazi I divided the kingdom between themselves, with Nur ad-Din governing Aleppo and Saif ad-Din Ghazi establishing himself in Mosul. The border between the two new kingdoms was formed by the Nahr al-Khabur River. Almo
2 CONC st as soon as he began his rule, Nur ad-Din attacked the Principality of Antioch, seizing several castles in the north of Syria, while at the same time he defeated an attempt by Joscelin II to recover the County of Edessa, which h
2 CONC ad been conquered by Zengi in 1144. (See Siege of Edessa.) In 1146, Nur ad-Din massacred the entire Christian population of the city and destroyed its fortifications,[1] in punishment for assisting Joscelin in this attempt. Althou
2 CONC gh according to Thomas Asbridge, the women and children of Edessa were enslaved.[2] He secured his hold on Antioch after crushing Raymond of Poitiers at the Battle of Inab in 1149, even presenting to the caliph, Raymond's severed 
2 CONC head and arms.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nur_ad-Din,_atabeg_of_Aleppo
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000216950669822&size=large}}
2 CONT [https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1f/Zengid_territory.png The Zengid state under Zengi in 1145, and expansion under Nur al-Din Zengi in 1174 CE - Source: The Atlas of the Crusades, p.59
2 CONT Author	naturalearthdata.com, offered to the Public Domain per Terms of Use]
