2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anna_Komnene
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Anna Komnene''', Latinized as Comnena (Greek: Άννα Κομνηνή, Anna Komnēnē; December 1, 1083–1153) was a Byzantine princess and scholar, '''daughter of the Byzantine emperor Alexios I Komnenos and Irene Doukaina'''. She wrote the
2 CONC  Alexiad, an account of her father's reign, making her one of the first Western female historians.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Family and early life==
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna was born in the Porphyra Chamber (the purple chamber) of the imperial palace of Constantinople and was thus a porphyrogenita. She was the eldest of nine children. Her younger siblings were (in order of birth) Maria Komnene, J
2 CONC ohn II Komnenos, Andronikos Komnenos, Isaac Komnenos, Eudokia Komnene, Theodora Komnene, Manuel Komnenos and Zoe Komnene.
2 CONT
2 CONT Although, she was carefully trained in the study of history, mathematics, science, and Greek philosophy, Anna’s parents banned her from studying ancient poetry (whose glorification of lustful gods and unchaste women they deemed in
2 CONC appropriate and even dangerous for a young woman of her class to study). Despite her parents' attempts to restrict her, Anna furtively studied the forbidden poetry with one of the imperial court’s eunuchs. Thus, Anna received an e
2 CONC xtraordinary education that undoubtedly made her one of the most learned women of her time.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Betrothal and marriage==
2 CONT
2 CONT As was customary for nobility in the medieval times, Anna was '''betrothed at infancy. She was to marry Constantine Doukas''', the son of Emperor Michael VII and Maria of Alania. Because at the time of the engagement Emperor Alexi
2 CONC os I had no rightful male heirs to inherit the throne, young Constantine was proclaimed the co-emperor of the Byzantine Empire. However, in 1087 a blood heir, John II, was born, and Constantine had to forfeit his imperial claims. 
2 CONC He died shortly thereafter.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1097, 14-year-old Anna Komnene married an accomplished young nobleman, the '''Caesar Nikephoros Bryennios the Younger'''. Nikephoros Bryennios was the son of an aristocratic family that had contested the throne before the acces
2 CONC sion of Alexios I. Nikephoros was also a renowned statesman, general, and historian. Anna claimed that the marriage was a political union rather than one of love. For the most part, however, it proved to be a successful union for 
2 CONC forty years, and produced four children—'''Alexios Komnenos, John Doukas, Irene Doukaina, and Maria Bryennaina Komnene'''.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Claim to the throne==
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1087, Anna’s brother, John II Komnenos, was born. Several years after his birth, in 1092, John was designated emperor.[1] According to Niketas Choniates, Emperor Alexios, Anna’s father, “favored” John and declared him emperor. 
2 CONC On the other hand, Anna’s mother, Irene Doukaina, according to Choniates “threw her full influence on the side of [Anna]” and “continually attempted” to persuade the emperor to designate Nikephoros Bryennios, Anna’s husband, as em
2 CONC peror.[2] Around 1112, Alexios fell sick with rheumatism and could not move. He therefore turned the civil government over to his wife, Irene Doukaina, who directed the administration to Anna’s husband, Nikephoros Bryennios.[3] As
2 CONC  Emperor Alexios lay dying in his imperial bedchamber, John, according to Choniates, arrived and “secretly” took the emperor’s ring from his father during an embrace “as though in mourning.”[4] In 1118, Alexios I Komnenos died.[5]
2 CONC  A clergy in Hagia Sophia acclaimed John emperor thereafter.[6]
2 CONT According to Dion C. Smythe, Anna “felt cheated” because she “should have inherited.”[7] Indeed, according to Anna Komnene in the Alexiad, at her birth she was presented with “a crown and imperial diadem.”[8] Anna’s “main aim” in 
2 CONC the depiction of events in the Alexiad, according to Vlada Stankovíc, was to “stress her own right” to the throne and “precedence over her brother, John.”[9] In view of this belief, Ellen Quandahl and Susan C. Jarratt record that 
2 CONC Anna was “almost certainly” involved in the murder plot against John at Alexius’s funeral.[10] Indeed, Anna, according to Barbara Hill, “attempted” to create military forces to depose John.[6] According to Choniates, Anna was “sti
2 CONC mulated by ambition and revenge” to scheme for the murder of her brother.[10] Dion C. Smythe states the plots “came to nothing.”[1] Ellen Quandahl and Susan C. Jarratt, record that, a short time afterward, Anna and Bryennios “orga
2 CONC nized another conspiracy.”[10] However, according to Barbara Hill, Bryennios “refused” to overthrow Alexios, making Anna unable to continue with her plans.[6] With this refusal, Anna, according to Choniates, exclaimed “that nature
2 CONC  had mistaken the two sexes and had endowed Bryennius [Bryennios] with the soul of a woman.” According to Ellen Quandahl and Susan C. Jarratt, Anna shows “a repetition of sexualized anger.”[10] Indeed, Dion C. Smythe asserts that 
2 CONC Anna’s goals were “thwarted by the men in her life.”[11] Irene, however, according to Hill, had declined to participate in plans to revolt against an “established” emperor.[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT Barbara Hill, however, points out that Choniates, whom the above sources draw upon, wrote after 1204, and accordingly was “rather far removed” from “actual” events and that his “agenda” was to “look for the causes” of the toppling
2 CONC  of Constantinople in 1204.[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT In the end, after her husband’s death, Anna went to a convent of Kecharitomene, which was founded by her mother, where she remained until her death.[12]
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Notes==
2 CONT
2 CONT # ^ a b Smythe 2006, p. 126.
2 CONT # ^ Choniates 1984, p. 5.
2 CONT # ^ Hill 2000, p. 46.
2 CONT # ^ Choniates 1984, p. 6.
2 CONT # ^ Smythe 2006, p. 127.
2 CONT # ^ a b c d e Hill 2000, p. 47.
2 CONT # ^ Smythe 1997, p. 241.
2 CONT # ^ Komnene 1969, p. 197.
2 CONT # ^ Stankovíc 2007, p. 174.
2 CONT # ^ a b c d Jarratt 2008, p. 308.
2 CONT # ^ Smythe 2006, p. 125.
2 CONT # ^ Jarratt 2008, p. 305.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==References==
2 CONT
2 CONT * Niketas Choniates, O City of Byzantium: Annals of Niketas Choniates, (Michigan: Wayne State University Press, 1984), 5-6.
2 CONT * Barbara Hill, “Actions speak louder than words: Anna Komnene’s attempted usurpation,” Anna Komnene and her times (2000): 46-47.
2 CONT * Susan C. Jarratt and Ellen Quandahl, ““To recall him…will be a subject of lamentation”: Anna Comnene as a rhetorical historiographer.” Rhetorica: A Journal of the History of Rhetoric (2000): 305-308, accessed April 21, 2011. htt
2 CONC p://www.jstor.org.ezproxy.library.wisc.edu/stable/pdfplus/10.1525/rh.2008.26.3.301.pdf?acceptTC=true.
2 CONT * Anna Komnene, The Alexiad, (New York: Penguin, 1969), 197.
2 CONT * Vlada Stankovíc, “Nikephoros Bryennios, Anna Komnene and Konstantios Doukas. A Story of Different Perspectives,” Byzantinische Zeitschrift (2007): 174.
2 CONT * Dion C. Smythe, “Middle Byzantine Family Values and Anna Komnene’s Alexiad,” Byzantine Women: Varieties of Experience (2006): 125-127.
2 CONT * Dion C. Symthe, “Outsiders by taxis perceptions of non-conformity eleventh and twelfth-century literature,” Byzantinische Forschungen: Internationale Zeitschrift für Byzantinistik (1997): 241.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Historian==
2 CONT
2 CONT In the seclusion of the monastery, Anna dedicated her time to studying philosophy and history. She held esteemed intellectual gatherings, including those dedicated to Aristotelian studies. Anna's intellectual genius and breadth of
2 CONC  knowledge is evident in her few works. Among other things, she was conversant with philosophy, literature, grammar, theology, astronomy, and medicine. It can be assumed because of minor errors that she may have quoted Homer and t
2 CONC he Bible from memory when writing her most celebrated work, the Alexiad. Her contemporaries, like the metropolitan Bishop of Ephesus, Georgios Tornikes, regarded Anna as a person who had reached "the highest summit of wisdom, both
2 CONC  secular and divine."
2 CONT
2 CONT Being a historian, Nikephoros Bryennios the Younger had been working on an essay that he called “Material For History,” which focused on the reign of Alexios I. He died in 1137 before finishing the work. At the age of 55, Anna too
2 CONC k it upon herself to finish her husband's work, calling the completed work the Alexiad, the history of her father's life and reign (1081–1118) in Greek. Alexiad is today the main source of Byzantine political history of the end of
2 CONC  the 11th century to the beginning of the 12th century.
2 CONT In the Alexiad, Anna provided insight on political relations and wars between Alexios I and the West. She vividly described weaponry, tactics and battles. It has been noted that she was writing about events that occurred when she 
2 CONC was a child, so these are not eye-witness accounts. Her neutrality is compromised by the fact that she was writing to praise her father and denigrate his successors. Despite her unabashed partiality, her account of the First Crusa
2 CONC de is of great value to history because it is the only Hellenic eyewitness account available. She had the opportunity to glean events from key figures in the Byzantine elite. Her husband Nikephorus Bryennios had fought in the clas
2 CONC h with crusade leader Godfrey of Bouillon outside Constantinople on Maundy Thursday 1097. Her uncle George Palaeologus was present at Pelekanon in June 1097 when Alexius I discussed future strategy with the crusaders. Thus the Ale
2 CONC xiad allows the events of the First Crusade to be seen from the Byzantine elite's perspective. It conveys the alarm felt at the scale of the western European forces proceeding through the Empire, and the dangers they may have pose
2 CONC d to the safety of Constantinople.
2 CONT
2 CONT Special suspicion was reserved for crusading leader Bohemond of Taranto, a southern Italian Norman who, under the leadership of his father Robert Guiscard, had invaded Byzantine territory in the Balkans in 1081. Though she conside
2 CONC rs him a barbarian and makes him the villain of her piece for his enmity with her father and his subsequent possession of formerly Byzantine Antioch, there is more than a hint of infatuation for this 'habitual rogue'.
2 CONT
2 CONT The book also contributes to understanding of the female mentality, mindset, and perception of the world during the Byzantine times.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna Komnene's literary style is fashioned after Thucydides, Polybius, and Xenophon. Consequently, it exhibits struggle for an Atticism characteristic of the period, whereby the resulting language is highly artificial. For the mos
2 CONC t part, the chronology of events in the Alexiad is sound, except for those that occurred after Anna’s exile to the monastery, when she no longer had access to the imperial archives. Nevertheless, her history meets the standards of
2 CONC  her time (Catholic Encyclopedia).
2 CONT
2 CONT The exact date of Anna Komnene’s death is uncertain. It is inferred from the Alexiad that she was still alive in 1148. Moreover, the Alexiad sheds light on Anna’s emotional turmoil. She wrote that no one could see her, yet many ha
2 CONC ted her (Lubarsky, pg 3). Thus, she loathed the isolated position in society that exile had forced upon her.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Depictions in fiction and other media==
2 CONT
2 CONT Fictional accounts of Anna Komnene’s life appear in the 1928 novel Anna Comnena by Naomi Mitchison and the 1999 novel for young people Anna of Byzantium by Tracy Barrett. A novel written in 2008 by the Albanian writer Ben Blushi c
2 CONC alled "Living on an island" also mentions her. The novel Аз, Анна Комнина (I, Anna Comnena) was written by Vera Mutafchieva, a Bulgarian writer and historian.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Family==
2 CONT
2 CONT By the Caesar Nikephoros Bryennios, Anna Komnene had several children, including:
2 CONT * Alexios Komnenos, megas doux, c. 1102–c. 1161/1167
2 CONT * John Doukas, c. 1103–after 1173
2 CONT * Irene Doukaina, c. 1105–?
2 CONT * Maria Bryennaina Komnene, c. 1107–?
2 CONT
