1 NAME Qiwam ad-Dawlah Abu Said Toghrul
2 GIVN Qiwam ad-Dawlah Abu Said Toghrul
1 NAME Toghrul, Sultan of Ghazna
1 SEX M
1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * from [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Toghrul_of_Ghazna '''Wikipedia''']
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Toghrul of Ghazna''' (full name: ''Qiwam ad-Dawlah Abu Said Toghrul''), was a Turkish slave general and usurper of the Ghaznavid throne. He was originally a ghulam in the service of the Ghaznavid Empire. Following his usurpatio
2 CONC n of the Ghaznavid throne from Abd al-Rashid and massacre of eleven Ghaznavid royal princes, he was known as the accursed, the inauspicious, the arrogant and the contemptible.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT =Life=
2 CONT
2 CONT Toghrul started his service as a ghulam of Sultan Mahmud and by the reign of Abd al-Rashid had risen to commander in chief of the army.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1042-43, Toghrul invaded Sistan with 2000 troops capturing a Saffarid family member Abu n-Nasr. Abu n-Nasr was taken back to Ghazna and later exchanged for a son of the Ghaznavid vizier, Ahmad b. Hasan Maimandi.[2] However, Tog
2 CONC hrul continued onward, occupying Karkuya and massacring both Muslim and Zoroastrian populations indiscriminately.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Toghrul led an army against Alp Arslan and won a victory at Hupyan in the Hindu Kush in the winter of 1051.[3] After defeating Alp Arslan, Toghrul marched to Sistan. He besieged the fortress of Taq and held it under siege for a mo
2 CONC nth, defeating a Seljuq relief army, commanded by Payghu, as well.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Unable to take Taq, Toghrul marched his army to Ghazna, sending letters to Abd al-Rashid asserting the disloyalty of the army. Rashid, filled with terror of a rebellion, locked himself up in the citadel. Upon his arrival, Toghrul 
2 CONC gained the support of the garrison, captured Abd al-Rashid, and had Abd al-Rashid and eleven other Ghaznavid princes executed.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT With Ghazna under his control, Toghrul sent letters to the ghulam general Kirghiz, commander of the Ghaznavid forces in India, seeking his support. Kirghiz responded by condemning Toghrul and his massacre of the Ghaznavid princes.
2 CONC  Meanwhile, Toghrul married Mas'ud I's daughter to legitimise his reign and started minting coins in his image.[5] Despite this, Kirghiz sent letters to the garrison and army commanders which motivated a ghulam named Nushtigin to 
2 CONC murder Toghrul. By the time Kirghiz and his army arrived, Toghrul's head was being paraded around Ghazna.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT =References=
2 CONT
2 CONT # C.E. Bosworth, The Later Ghaznavids, (Columbia University Press, 1977), 41.
2 CONT # C.E. Bosworth, The Later Ghaznavids, 29.
2 CONT # C.E. Bosworth, The Later Ghaznavids, 43.
2 CONT # 'Izz al-D in Ibn al-Athir, The Annals of the Saljuq Turks, transl. D.S. Richards, ed. Carole Hillenbrand, (Routledge, 2002), 83,note47.
2 CONT # C.E. Bosworth, The Later Ghaznavids, 45-46.
