1 OCCU Empress consort of Trebizond c. 1240s – 1250s
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Probably an illegitimate daughter of Lasha Georgi. However, it is argued that she could have been no more than a commoner, so we actally no nothing of her ancestry.  [Rusudan is commonly considered a member of the 
2 CONC Bagrationi dynasty.[1] Szabolcs de Vajay has argued that Rusudan was probably an illegitimate daughter of Giorgi IV Lasha.[2] However, Michel Kuršanskis argues that Rusudan could have been no more than a commoner and mistress of E
2 CONC mperor Manuel. First, Kuršanskis notes that it was not Georgian custom to name their children after their parents. "That alone should be enough to indicate that Rusudan couldn't be a daughter of the homonymous Queen," he writes. H
2 CONC e points out that in the chronicle of Michael Panaretos she is the only one of the three women who gave him children who is not referred to as kyra, or "lady". Lastly, Rusudan is described simply as "from Iberia" and nothing is sa
2 CONC id about her ancestry Empress Rusudan is mentioned briefly in the chronicle of Michael Panaretos. "Lady Theodora Komnene, the first daughter of lord Manuel the Grand Komnenos by Russadan from Iberia." [4] Drawing on Georgian sourc
2 CONC es, Kuršanskis suggests that Rusudan died in 1247.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT Manuel had at least two daughters whose mother is not mentioned, and they could be children by Rusudan. One of the daughters married Demetre II of Georgia, the other married one of his Didebul.[6] Though mentioned in modern geneal
2 CONC ogies as a name, "Didebul" was actually a title. According to "The Bagrationi (Bagration) Dynasty" by Christopher Buyers, the Didebul were "non-hereditary noblemen of high rank, senior to aznaur, usually enjoyed by one in state se
2 CONC rvice".] Ref: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rusudan_of_Georgia,_Empress_of_Trebizond
2 CONT
2 CONT ===
2 CONT
2 CONT Theodora Megale Komnene (Greek: Θεοδώρα Μεγάλη Κομνηνή, Theodōra Megalē Komnēnē), (before 1253– after 1285), Empress of Trebizond from 1284 to 1285. She was a daughter of Emperor Manuel I of Trebizond by his second wife, Rusudan, 
2 CONC a Georgian princess.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1284, with help of Georgian King of Imereti, David VI Narin she managed to seize the crown from her half-brother, Emperor John II. Shortly afterwards she was defeated and John regained his throne, but she had managed reign long
2 CONC  enough to have minted her own coins. Either before or after her short reign, Theodora was a nun.
2 CONT
2 CONT W. Miller, Trebizond: The Last Greek Empire of the Byzantine Era, Chicago, 1926.
