1 NOTE {geni:about_me} === Isma'il ibn Ja'far 
2 CONT * romanized: Ismāʿīl ibn Jaʿfar al-Mubārak
2 CONT * Arabic:  إسْماعِيل ٱبْن جَعْفَر ٱلْمُبَارَك,
2 CONT * Born	c. 719/722 CE  (100/103 AH)
2 CONT * Medina Died	c. 765/775 CE  (148/158 AH)
2 CONT * Ja'far al-Sadiq (father)
2 CONT * Fatima bint al-Husayn al-Athram ibn al-Hasan ibn Ali (mother)
2 CONT * Spouse 
2 CONT Children	
2 CONT # Muhammad
2 CONT # Ali
2 CONT # Fatima
2 CONT
2 CONT Progenitor of ''Ismaili Imamate'' (''Ismailism'').
2 CONT
2 CONT '''6th Shia Ismai'li Imaam'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * Date of Ascension: 15th Shawwal 148 AH (7th December 765 AD)
2 CONT * Period of Imamate: 10 Years
2 CONT * Wilaadat (Birth): Shawwal 100 or 103 AH (April 719 or March 722 AD)
2 CONT * Wafaat (Death): c. 137 AH or 158 AH (c. 755 AD or 775 AD)
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT The Shi'a faced persecution under the first two dynasties that rules over the Muslim community: the Ummayad caliphate and the Abbassid caliphate. It was in this climate of persecution during the early Abbasid period tha the Imamis
2 CONC  ( the Imamis believed that their imams were appointed by hereditary designation (nass) from among each imam's male descendants, usually his eldest son) were divided flollowing Imam Jafar's al-Sadiq's death in 765. Most hisorical 
2 CONC evidence suggests that Imam Jafar al-Sadiq had designated his son Ismail as the next imam. However, there was confusion as to Ismail's whereabout at the time of his father's death. Some believed that he had died before his father,
2 CONC  while others claimed that he was alive but hiding from Abbasid persecution. Many Shi'a eventually rallied around Ismail's younger brother, Musa al-Kazim as their imam. These individuals, later known as the Ithna asharis (Twelvers
2 CONC ) followed the descendants of Musa al-Kazim until their twelfth imam disappeared in what they believed to be a spiritual occultation (ghayba).
2 CONT
2 CONT There were 2 groups that would form the earliest Ismailis. The first believed that \Ismail was alive and in hiding, thus acknowledging him as their imam. The second group accepted that Ismail had died , but argued that since he wa
2 CONC s given the nass from his father , the imamate should remain among his descendants. Ultimately both groups came to accept Ismail's son Muhammad as imam. ref. A short history of the Ismailis - Farhad Daftary < [https://www.ismaili.
2 CONC net/histoire/history04/history404.html link] 
2 CONT
2 CONT Twin brother of [http://www.geni.com/people/Abdullah-al-Aftah-bin-Imaam-Ja-afar-us-
2 CONT Saadiq/6000000003796438051/ Sayyidi 'Abdullah al-Aftah bin Imaam Ja'afar us-Saadiq]
2 CONT
2 CONT Source 1: Sahifa
2 CONT
2 CONT Source 2: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Isma%27il_ibn_Jafar
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Isma‘il ibn Ja‘far''' (Arabic: إسماعيل بن جعفر‎; c. Born:Shawwal 100 AH/719 AD) was the eldest son of Imam Ja'far al-Sadiq, and he was the full-brother of Abdullah al-Aftah. Following Ja'far's death, the Shia community split be
2 CONC tween the element that would become the Twelver Shia, and those who believed the Imamate passed through to Ismail's son; the Ismaili branch of Shia Islam is accordingly named for Ismail.[1] According to both the Nizari and Mustaal
2 CONC i Shia sects, he is the sixth Imam. He was buried in Jannat al-Baqi
2 CONT
2 CONT NOTES BY MULLA SA'DULLAH HUSAMI -15th Shawwaal 1436 Hijri.
2 CONT
2 CONT   ، الإمام جعفر بن محمد الصادق - ولد عام 80 في المدينة المنورة .و نال الإمامة بعد والدہ عام 114 ، بلغ عدد طلابه 4000 طالب منهم أبو حنيفة النعمان صاحب المذهب الحنفي الذي قال : لولا السنتان لهلك النعمان - يقصد بهما العامين التي درس 
2 CONC فيهما على يد الإمام الصادق - له عليہ السلام العديد من المؤلفات التي نشرت عن طريق تلميذه وبابه المفضل بن عمرو الجعفي المنتقل عام 180 - منها مصباح الشريعة و المخصوص و الصراط المستقيم و الاشباح والاظلة و توحيد المفضل و الأبتداء والأن
2 CONC تهاء وقد يسمى البدأ والجزاء- كان الإمام عليہ السلام صاحب فكرة التقويم الفاطمي الذي قد مر منه منذ هجرة الرسول حتى يومنا هذا ست دورات قمرية كبرى كل دورة = 210 سنة حتى عام 1260 سنة ومن عام 1261 دخلت الدورة السابعة الكبرى ونحن فيها حت
2 CONC ى عام 1470 وهي عبارة عن سبع دورات صغرى كل دورة = 30 سنة ومع عام 1441 ندخل الدورة الصغرى السابعة والأخيرة للدورة السابعة الكبرى والتي تستمر حتى عام 1470 ۔ وكانت نقله الإمام  الصادق عام 148 بالسم من ضده أبو جعفر المنصور الدوانيقي لع
2 CONC نه الله تعالى ➖
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT '''6th Fatimid Imaam''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT * ''Imaamate Period/ Reign: 34 years; 95-95 AH (714- AD)''
2 CONT * Wilaadat (Birth): 
2 CONT * Wafaat (Death):  95 AH (714 AD)
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Ismâ`il ben Ja`far (arabe إسماعيل بن جعفر ismā`īl ben ja`far) est né à Médine entre 719 et 722. Fils aîné du sixième imam chiite (Jafar as-Sâdiq), il est probablement mort avant son père en laissant un héritier son fils Muhammad. 
2 CONC Ses partisans créèrent la Dawa des ismaéliens contre son frère cadet Mûsa qui devint le septième imâm à la tête des imâmites.
2 CONT
2 CONT Les califes abbassides considéraient les imâms chiites comme de dangereux concurrents sur le plan religieux et comme des adversaires politiques potentiels. Les imâms étaient donc étroitement surveillés. Le calife al-Mansûr essaya 
2 CONC d’inviter Ja`far et son fils Isma`il à Bagdad dans le but de les surveiller plus étroitement. Ismâ`il étant le successeur désigné, son père le tenait à l’écart de ses autres enfants pour le protéger. Lors d’absences de Ja`far à Mé
2 CONC dine c’était Ismâ`il qui tenait le rôle d’imâm.
2 CONT
2 CONT Il circule de nombreuses versions de sa disparition. La plus simple dit qu’il serait mort en 762 trois ans avant son père. Une autre affirme que l’enterrement qui eut lieu en 762 n’était qu’une feinte pour échapper à la vindicte d
2 CONC u calife. Dans cette hypothèse, Ismâ`il serait mort en 775 dix ans après son père. Il aurait terminé sa vie en restant caché et il aurait donné à son fils Muhammad al-Maktûm l’ordre d’administrer la communauté en son nom. Une autr
2 CONC e c'est qu'il aurait disparu, qu'il n'est pas mort mais occulté.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT [...The sixth imam, Ja'far al-Sadiq, had appointed (naṣṣ) his son Isma'il ibn Ja'far as his successor, but Isma'il died before his father, and when al-Sadiq himself died in 765, the succession was left open. One faction of Shi'a h
2 CONC eld that al-Sadiq had designated another son, Musa al-Kazim, as his heir. Others followed other sons, Muhammad and Abdallah al-Aftah—after his death shortly after they went over to Musa's camp—or even refused to believe that al-Sa
2 CONC diq had died, and expected his return as a messiah.[2] Musa's adherents, who constituted the majority of al-Sadiq's followers, followed his line down to the twelfth imam, Muhammad al-Mahdi, who vanished in 874.[1][3] Another branc
2 CONC h considered that Ja'far was followed by a seventh imam, who also had gone into hiding; hence this party is known as the Seveners. The exact identity of that seventh imam was disputed, but by the late 9th century had commonly been
2 CONC  identified with Muhammad, son of Isma'il and grandson of al-Sadiq. 
2 CONT
2 CONT From Muhammad's father, Isma'il, the sect receives its name of "Isma'ili".[1][4][5] Neither Isma'il's nor Muhammad's lives are well known, and ater Muhammad's death during the reign of Harun al-Rashid (r. 786–809, the history of t
2 CONC he early Isma'ili movement becomes obscure.[..] re: Shi'isme in the 9th century  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abdallah_al-Mahdi_Billah
