2 CONT '''References and further reading on the Kingdom of Abkhazia'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * (English) Alexei Zverev, Ethnic Conflicts in the Caucasus 1988-1994, in B. Coppieters (ed.), Contested Borders in the Caucasus, Brussels: VUBPress, 1996
2 CONT * Graham Smith, Edward A Allworth, Vivien A Law, Annette Bohr, Andrew Wilson, Nation-Building in the Post-Soviet Borderlands: The Politics of National Identities, Cambridge University Press (September 10, 1998), ISBN 0-521-59968-7
2 CONT * Encyclopaedia of Islam
2 CONT * (English) Center for Citizen Peacebuilding, Aspects of the Georgian-Abkhazian Conflict
2 CONT * S. H. Rapp, Studies In Medieval Georgian Historiography: Early Texts And Eurasian Contexts, Peeters Bvba (September 25, 2003) ISBN 90-429-1318-5
2 CONT * (English) Conflicting Narratives in Abkhazia and Georgia. Different Visions of the Same History and the Quest for Objectivity, an article by Levan Gigineishvili, 2003
2 CONT * (English) The Role of Historiography in the Abkhazo-Georgian Conflict, an article by Seiichi Kitagawa, 1996
2 CONT * (English) History of Abkhazia. Medieval Abkhazia: 620-1221 by Andrew Andersen
2 CONT * Georgiy I Mirsky, G I Mirskii, On Ruins of Empire: Ethnicity and Nationalism in the Former Soviet Union (Contributions in Political Science), Greenwood Press (January 30, 1997) ISBN 0-313-30044-5
2 CONT * Ronald Grigor Suny, The Making of the Georgian Nation: 2nd edition (December 1994), Indiana University Press, ISBN 0-253-20915-3, page 45
2 CONT * Robert W. Thomson (translator), Rewriting Caucasian History: The Medieval Armenian Adaptation of the Georgian Chronicles: The Original Georgian Texts and Armenian Adaptation (Oxford Oriental Monographs), Oxford University Press,
2 CONC  USA (June 27, 1996), ISBN 0-19-826373-2
2 CONT * Toumanoff C., Chronology of the Kings of Abasgia and other Problems // Le Museon, 69 (1956), S. 73-90.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Constantine_III_of_Abkhazia
2 CONT
2 CONT Constantine III of Abkhazia From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia Constantine III of Abkhazia, a member of the House of the Anosids, was King of Abkhazia after Bagrat I, from 898/99 to 916/17 AD.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kingdom_of_Abkhazia Kingdom of Abkhazia The Kingdom of Abkhazia (Georgian: აფხაზეთის სამეფო; Aphkhazetis Samepo), also known as the Kingdom of the Abkhazes (აფხაზთა სამეფო) refers to an early medieval 
2 CONC feudal state in the Caucasus which lasted from the 780s until being united, through dynastic succession, with the Kingdom of Georgia (see Tao-Klarjeti) in 1008.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abkhazia Abkhazia (Abkhaz: Аҧсны́ Apsny [apʰsˈnɨ]; Georgian: აფხაზეთი Apkhazeti; Russian: Абхазия Abkhaziya) is a disputed territory and partially recognised state controlled by a separatist government
2 CONC  on the eastern coast of the Black Sea and the south-western flank of the Caucasus.
