1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * From [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ala_ad-Din_Tekish '''Wikipedia'''], the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Ala ad-Din Tekish''' (Persian: علاء الدين تكش; full name: Ala ad-Dunya wa ad-Din Abul Muzaffar Tekish ibn Il-Arslan) or Tekesh or Takesh was the Shah of Khwarezmian Empire from 1172 to 1200. He was the son of Il-Arslan. His rul
2 CONC e was contested by his brother, Sultan Shah, who held a principality in Khorasan. Tekish inherited Sultan Shah's state after he died in 1193. In Turkic the name Tekish means he who strikes in battle.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1194 Tekish defeated the Seljuq sultan of Hamadan, Toghrul III, in an alliance with Caliph Al-Nasir, and conquered his territories. After the war, he broke with the Caliphate and was on the brink of a war with it until the Cali
2 CONC ph accepted him as Sultan of Iraq, Khorasan, and Turkestan in 1198.
2 CONT
2 CONT He died of a peritonsillar abscess in 1200[2] and was succeeded by his son, Ala ad-Din Muhammad.
2 CONT
2 CONT =References=
2 CONT
2 CONT # "TEKIŠ B. IL ARSLĀN" Encyclopædia Iranica. Retrieved 30 August 2014
2 CONT # Juvaini, Ala-ad-Din Ata-Malik, History of the World Conqueror, Manchester University Press, Manchester, 1997. p. 314.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Sources=
2 CONT
2 CONT * Bosworth, C. E. (1968). "The Political and Dynastic History of the Iranian World (A.D. 1000–1217)". In Frye, R. N. The Cambridge History of Iran, Volume 5: The Saljuq and Mongol periods. Cambridge: Cambridge University Press. pp
2 CONC . 1–202. ISBN 0-521-06936-X.
2 CONT * 
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Ala ad-Din Tekish (Persian: علاء الدين تكش; full name: Ala ad-Dunya wa ad-Din Abul Muzaffar Tekish ibn Il-Arslan) or Tekesh or Takesh was the Shah of Khwarezmian Empire from 1172 to 1200. He was the son of Il-Arslan. His rule was 
2 CONC contested by his brother, Sultan Shah, who held a principality in Khorasan. Tekish inherited Sultan Shah's state after he died in 1193. In Turkic the name Tekish means he who strikes in battle.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1194 Tekish defeated the Seljuq sultan of Hamadan, Toghrul III, in an alliance with Caliph Al-Nasir, and conquered his territories. After the war, he broke with the Caliphate and was on the brink of a war with it until the Cali
2 CONC ph accepted him as Sultan of Iraq, Khorasan, and Turkestan in 1198.
2 CONT
2 CONT He died of a peritonsillar abscess in 1200 and was succeeded by his son, Ala ad-Din Muhammad.
