1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * Inanj Sonqur and his daughter are mentioned in [http://www.liquisearch.com/military_history_of_ray_iran/last_days_of_the_seljuqs_late_12th_century '''Military History of Ray, Iran - Last Days of The Seljuqs, Late
2 CONC  12th Century''']
2 CONT
2 CONT The rivalry after the death of Malik-Shah I had split the Sultanate, effectively creating buffer states on all sides of Ray. Nevertheless, when the Jewish traveller Rabbi Benjamin of Tudela visited the region around 1169 CE, he wa
2 CONC s told that some 15 years earlier an Oghuz tribe called the Khofar-al-Turak had allied themselves with some of the lost tribes of Israel, who lived among the mountains near Nishapur, to invade the heartland of Iran, capturing and 
2 CONC looting the city of Ray. This would refer to the Oghuz incursions of 548 H (1153-4 CE) from their settlements east of Khorasan, which led to their defeat of Sultan Ahmed Sanjar, although other records of these events do not specif
2 CONC ically mention Ray. There were other threats to the city at this period, though. Ildegiz, atabeg of Azerbaijan, was determined to oust the amir of Ray, Inanj Sonqur, who had challenged his attempt to install his stepson Arslan-Sha
2 CONC h (Arslan ibn Toghril, grandson of Muhammad) as Sultan of Iran. In 555 H (1160 CE) he succeeded, and Inanj withdrew to Bisotun. Inanj did not give up, and in 562 H (1166-7 CE) he sought help from the Khwarazm Shah, Il-Arslan. This
2 CONC  alliance succeeded in recapturing Ray from the atabeg's garrison, but the Khwarazmians caused much damage in nearby Abhar and Qazwin before they returned home. Ildegiz arranged for Inanj to be killed, and put his son and heir Muh
2 CONC ammad ibn Ildigiz Jahan Pahlawan (husband of Inanj's daughter Inanj-Khatun) in charge at Ray. After his father's death in 568 H (1172-3 CE), Pahlawan, as atabeg, was also the power behind the Sultan's throne, and became the key to
2 CONC  stability when Arslan-Shah was assassinated in 571 H (1176 CE), leaving a son and heir, Toghril III, just seven years old.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT * From [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%C3%8Fnanch_Sonqur '''Wikipedia'''], the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Ïnanch Sonqur''' (died 1169) was the amir of Ray from 1160 at the latest until his death.[1] During his eight years in power he played a major role in the events that occurred in northern Iran.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Career=
2 CONT
2 CONT Before the decline of the Seljuk Sultanate of Merv, the city of Ray had been part of Sultan Sanjar's territories. After Sanjar's capture in 1153 by bands of rebellious Oghuz Turks, his empire fell into chaos. In the midst of this 
2 CONC confused situation, Ïnanch Sonqur was able to take Ray with assistance from the Bavandids.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Ïnanch soon began meddling in the affairs of the Seljuks of Hamadan. After the death of Sultan Muhammad ibn Mahmud in 1159, the amirs of the surrounding provinces disagreed on who should succeed him. In the end, Ïnanch's candidate
2 CONC  Suleiman Shah won out.[3] For the duration of Suleiman Shah's reign Ïnanch lent him support, but the sultan's incompetence eventually led the amirs to overthrow him and ask the Atabeg of Azerbaijan, Shams al-Din Eldigüz, to repla
2 CONC ce him with Arslan.
2 CONT
2 CONT Arslan was accordingly installed in Hamadan in 1161. Eldigüz was able to gain Ïnanch's approval of the affair after arranging a marriage between his son Pahlavan and Ïnanch's daughter, Ïnanch Khatun.[3] Arslan, however, ran into p
2 CONC roblems of his own, and eventually Ïnanch and a coalition of other amirs revolted and marched against Hamadan. Arslan, Eldigüz and Gird-Bazu defeated them in battle, forcing Ïnanch to flee.
2 CONT
2 CONT After his defeat, Ïnanch made his way to the territories of the Bavandids. He then went to the court of the Shah of Khwarezm, Il-Arslan, who supplied him with an army and sent him back to northern Iran.[4] Ïnanch invaded the area 
2 CONC around Qazvin and Zanjan in 1166/7, but was unable to make any headway against the Seljuks or Eldigüz, and the loosely disciplined Khwarezmian troops were hated by the local people. Ïnanch retreated to Gurgan, but was able to enli
2 CONC st the support of the Bavandids and with their help managed to regain Ray.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT Ïnanch's victory in reasserting his position was short lived. In 1169 Eldigüz invaded and besieged Ïnanch. Soon afterwards, he convinced some of Ïnanch's ghulams to murder him. Ray was taken by Eldigüz, who granted it to his son P
2 CONC ahlavan and left his vizier to administer the city.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT =Notes=
2 CONT
