1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Founder of the kingdom of Artsakh
2 CONT
2 CONT The Kingdom of Artsakh (Armenian: Արցախի թագավորություն) was a medieval dependent Armenian kingdom on the territory of Syunik, Artsakh (present-day Nagorno-Karabakh), Gardman and Gegharkunik.[1] Contemporary sources referred to it
2 CONC  as the Khachen. The royal[citation needed] house of Khachen was a cadet branch of the ancient Syunid dynasty and was named Khachen, after its main stronghold. The kingdom emerged when Hovhannes-Senekerim acquired the royal title 
2 CONC in 1000.[citation needed]
2 CONT
2 CONT The kingdom was under the protectorate of the Bagratuni kings of Armenia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Artsakh maintained its sovereign rulers, though in the early 13th century they accepted Georgian, then Mongol suzerainty.[2] They lost the royal title after the assassination of Hasan-Jalal (1214–1261) by the Ilkhanid ruler Arghun
2 CONC , but continued to rule Syunik as a principality, which from the 16th century comprised five Armenian melikdoms and lasted until the early 19th century.[1] The descendants of the kings of Syunik played a prominent role in the hist
2 CONC ory of Syunik as far as the 20th century.[citation needed]
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sahak_Sevada#:~:text=Sahak%20Sevada%20%28Armenian%3A%20%D5%8D%D5%A1%D5%B0%D5%A1%D5%AF%20%D5%8D%D5%A5%D6%82%D5%A1%D5%A4%D5%A1%D5%B5%2C%20romanized%20%3A%20Sahak,well%20as%20part%20of%20Utik%20-Gardman%
2 CONC 20and%20Dzoroget.
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT Sahak Sevada
2 CONT Սահակ Սևադա
2 CONT Prince of Gardman
2 CONT Reign	895 – 940
2 CONT Predecessor	Grigol
2 CONT Dynasty	Arranshahik
2 CONT Religion	Monophysitism
2 CONT Sahak Sevada (Armenian: Սահակ Սեւադայ, romanized: Sahak Sewaday[a]) was ruler of Gardman between 895–940 and father-in-law of King Ashot II of Armenia. He was the son of Grigol Hamam and brother of Atrnerseh, ruler of Hereti.[1] H
2 CONC is possessions covered Parisos, a district of Artsakh, as well as part of Utik-Gardman and Dzoroget.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT In Book III of the medieval Armenian text The History of the Country of Albania, Sahak Sevada is described as a "brave and capable man" who was a "lover of letters and appointed writers to his household." The author adds that "Smb
2 CONC at, king of Armenia, fought against him [Sahak Sevada] with zeal and skill, but could not bring him to submission."[2] Sahak's contemporary, Catholicos Hovhannes Draskhanakerttsi, describes him as a man of "great wisdom."[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT The author of The History of the Country of Albania writes that Sahak Sevada had two sons, Grigor and Davit, and several grandsons and great-grandsons through Grigor, one of whom, John Senekerim, later became ruler of Hereti.[2] H
2 CONC istorian Bagrat Ulubabyan considers it more likely that John Senekerim was Sahak Sevada's own son.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Sahak Sevada married one of his daughters to Ashot II, ruler of the Bagratid Kingdom of Armenia. His other daughter Shahandukht was married to Smbat, ishkhan (later king) of Syunik. Sahak aided King Ashot II in an unsuccessful ass
2 CONC ault against the king's cousin and pretender to the throne, also named Ashot, and in crushing a revolt by Prince Movses of Utik.[3] However, as a result of political changes, Sahak sided with Smbat and revolted against Ashot II an
2 CONC d declared independence. During the battle, Sahak and his eldest son Grigor were captured and blinded by Ashot, after which Gardman was likely made a royal domain of the Bagratuni kings.[1][3][5]
